1: # The starred cap and the finite generic source reduction
2: 
3: ## P3K.21. Proved: the starred-fibre cap obstruction and the remaining generic pairs
4: 
5: In the established sphere orbit and root-line cap scope, a
6: nonzero-linearization \(I_s^*\) source fibre can occur only
7: with \(s=1\), a primitive quadratic cap, and
8: \[
9:                          a\equiv0\pmod2,
10:                          \qquad b\equiv2\pmod4.       \tag{P3K.21.1}
11: \]
12: The two \(I_3^*\) rank-one source configurations are excluded.
13: Combined with P3K.19–20 and the actual source table, the
14: remaining generic source coefficients are the 26 pairs in
15: the 16 profiles displayed below.  This is a proved finite
16: restriction on the generic source data; it does not declare
17: all remaining affine cap tuples to be sphere tuples without
18: their integral gluing calculation.
19: 
20: **Proof: the marked quadratic root.** The quadratic root of
21: \(I_s^*\) is the Tate \(I_{2s}\) fibre.  Its deck
22: acts on component labels by \(j\mapsto-j\), since
23: it is elliptic inversion and fixes the zero component.
24: The two invariant components are \(j=0,s\).  On each,
25: inversion has two fixed smooth points.  Near such a point
26: the two local coordinates have signs \((-1,-1)\), so
27: the quotient has equation \(v^2=uq\).  Resolving it
28: adds a multiplicity-one outer component.  The quotient
29: of the cycle is a path of \(s+1\) multiplicity-two
30: components, with two such outer leaves at each end.
31: This is the actual affine \(D_{s+4}\) graph.
32: 
33: The end \(j=0\) contains the zero section.  Remove
34: its corresponding outer leaf to obtain the finite
35: \(D_{s+4}\) intersection matrix.  Its inverse has
36: diagonal entry one at the other leaf of that end, and
37: \((s+4)/4\) at the two far-end leaves.  These entries
38: follow directly from the graph matrix; they are also the
39: local-height entries used in P3F.2.  The primitive
40: rank-one generator meets a spinor leaf, so its quadratic
41: root component is exactly \(s\), not zero.
42: The further degree-\(m/2\) root multiplies component
43: labels by \(m/2\).  Thus on an even \(m\)-root
44: the source fibre is \(I_{sm}\), the generator has
45: component \(sm/2\), and the canonical deck still
46: acts by reflection \(j\mapsto-j\).
47: 
48: An odd root leaves a starred fibre with unequal
49: multiplicities, excluded by the reduced-root conclusion
50: of P3K.19.  For an even root write the source component
51: action of the affine cap map as
52: \(A(j)=-j+\alpha\).  The suspension map acts as
53: \(B(j)=j+bsm/2\).  Its root fibre is singular, so
54: P3K.19 makes the pair \((m,n)\) primitive.  In
55: particular \(n\) is odd.  Since \(A^m\) is the
56: identity on components, the actual norm relation
57: \(A^m=B^n\) forces \(b\) even.  Then \(B\)
58: has trivial component action.  A reflection has orbits
59: of size at most two; transitivity forces \(sm\le2\).
60: Hence \(s=1,m=2\), and \(\alpha\) is odd.
61: The multidegree-zero root line has generic component
62: \(asm/2\); its component-Laplacian equation forces
63: \(a\) even, as in P3K.19.
64: 
65: There is one further unit constraint, which the component
66: labels alone would miss.  Write the quadratic Tate period
67: as \(Q_T=z^2u(z^2)\) and the generator as
68: \(Z_G=zv(z)\).  Its actual descent under inversion says
69: \(Z_G(-z)=Q_T/Z_G(z)\).  Thus
70: \(v(z)v(-z)=-u(z^2)\), and the identity-component
71: limit of \(2G\) is exactly \(-1\).  For the
72: affine reflection write
73: \(A(Z,z)=(R(z)/Z,-z)\), where
74: \(R=z^\alpha w(z)\).  Its square multiplies
75: \(Z\) by \(R(-z)/R(z)\), whose central value is
76: \((-1)^\alpha=-1\).  The central value of the
77: right-hand side \(B^n\) is
78: \((-1)^{bn/2}\).  Since \(n\) is odd, equality
79: forces \(b\equiv2\pmod4\).  This proves (P3K.21.1)
80: with both the component and unit parts of the norm retained.
81: 
82: **Proof: the finite generic factor list.** A simple whole
83: zero requires an \(I_1\) source value and \(ab=d\)
84: by P3K.20.  The three profiles with no \(I_1\) are
85: therefore removed.  P3K.19 imposes \(N\mid a\)
86: and \(\gcd(N,b)=1\) at every multiplicative fibre.
87: The preceding argument removes both \(I_3^*\) profiles
88: and imposes (P3K.21.1) at \(I_1^*\).  Enumerating the
89: positive divisors of each of the eight height denominators
90: now gives exactly the following list.
91: 
92: | OS row | Source fibre profile | Remaining \((a,b)\) |
93: |---|---|---|
94: |43|\(III^*+3I_1\)|\((1,2),(2,1)\)|
95: |43|\(III^*+II+I_1\)|\((1,2),(2,1)\)|
96: |45|\(I_8+4I_1\)|\((8,1)\)|
97: |45|\(I_8+II+2I_1\)|\((8,1)\)|
98: |47|\(I_7+I_2+3I_1\)|\((14,1)\)|
99: |47|\(I_7+I_2+II+I_1\)|\((14,1)\)|
100: |47|\(I_7+III+2I_1\)|\((7,2),(14,1)\)|
101: |49|\(IV^*+I_2+2I_1\)|\((2,3),(6,1)\)|
102: |49|\(IV^*+III+I_1\)|\((1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1)\)|
103: |50|\(I_1^*+I_3+2I_1\)|\((6,2)\)|
104: |50|\(I_1^*+IV+I_1\)|\((2,6),(6,2)\)|
105: |55|\(I_5+I_4+3I_1\)|\((20,1)\)|
106: |55|\(I_5+I_4+II+I_1\)|\((20,1)\)|
107: |56|\(I_5+I_3+I_2+2I_1\)|\((30,1)\)|
108: |56|\(I_5+I_3+III+I_1\)|\((15,2),(30,1)\)|
109: |56|\(I_5+IV+I_2+I_1\)|\((10,3),(30,1)\)|
110: 
111: The script `code/part3_fourfold/source_reduction.py` starts
112: from all 21 source profiles of P3F.1, retains their marked
113: height denominators, and executes these exact tests.  It
114: also exports the affine \(D\) matrices and their inverse
115: entries, so the starred marking is independently checkable.
116: 
117: **Proof: the indispensable vertical correction in row 50.**
118: The list allows arbitrary vertical line divisors, as the
119: literal construction does.  For its two row-50 factors,
120: the necessary correction is explicit.  Label the affine
121: \(D_5\) fibre by central vertices \(C_0,C_1\),
122: identity outer leaf \(L_0\), its order-two mate \(L_1\),
123: and spinor leaves \(R_0,R_1\).  The two left leaves
124: meet \(C_0\), the two right leaves meet \(C_1\),
125: and \(C_0,C_1\) meet.  Translation by \(2G\) or
126: \(6G\) swaps \(L_0,L_1\) and \(R_0,R_1\),
127: while fixing the two central components.
128: 
129: For \(a=2\) or \(6\), the section \(aG\) meets
130: \(L_1\), so the bare section-line degree vector is
131: \(e_{L_1}-e_{L_0}\).  If \(B_D\) is the actual
132: affine intersection matrix, then
133: \[
134:  e_{L_1}-e_{L_0}-B_D e_{L_0}
135:                   =e_{L_0}+e_{L_1}-e_{C_0}.          \tag{P3K.21.2}
136: \]
137: The right-hand side is invariant under the stated
138: translation.  Thus the actual line
139: \(\mathcal O(aG-O-L_0)\), with \(L_0\) here a
140: vertical fibre component, has the required invariant
141: component degrees.  At the other reducible fibre the
142: displayed factor pairs likewise have invariant component
143: degrees: at \(I_3\) the line point is in the identity
144: component; at \(IV\), either the line point is in the
145: identity component or the translation preserves all
146: components.  The Hom ratio is generically trivial and
147: has degree zero on every fibre component.  Its vertical
148: divisor is therefore a sum of whole fibres, and P3K.14
149: gives its base degree one.  Choosing its section places
150: the simple zero at the selected \(I_1\).
151: 
152: This proves that these corrected source line data satisfy
153: the literal whole-fibre Hom condition.  It does not
154: replace the remaining integral cap/gluing verification
155: for their total threefolds.
156: **End of proof.**
157: 
158: The 26 pairs are a finite necessary source reduction in
159: the declared orbit and cap scope.  The full cap data still
160: include their exact cyclic norm, logarithmic site, integral
161: delta collar, Seifert determinant, and integral topology.
162: No unverified pair in this table is assigned a sphere or
163: a return index solely by this arithmetic list.
